          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "**E11 is a family of codes, and E11.9 is one member of it.** According to the coding summary \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories,\" E11 is never used on its own at three characters. The characters after it name a complication, such as kidney involvement, eye involvement, nerve damage, circulation problems in the limbs, or high or low blood sugar. Diabetes documented without complications has its own code ending in 9, which is E11.9.\n\n**When E11.9 fits:** The same document says E11.9 is the code for type 2 diabetes documented without complications. The passages don't spell out the full decision rules, so check the complete coding guidance before relying on this.\n\n**Insulin:** The same summary says that a person taking insulin is still coded to E11. Long-term insulin use is recorded with a separate status code.\n\n**What the results did not cover:** The retrieved passages do not explain how to choose between E11.9 and a complication code when a record mentions both, or how the \"with\" convention and Excludes notes apply to diabetes. A general passage on code conventions (\"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\") mentions these rules, but the excerpt cuts off before it explains them. Confirm the final code with a trained medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
